# Pinned scanner container images.
#
# Digests, not tags. `:stable` and `:latest` make a scan's verdict a function of
# when the job ran rather than of the tree it scanned — the same reason
# `.openspec-version` exists at the repo root for the OpenSpec CLI. A scanner
# that silently changes underneath you cannot be bisected against, and a rule set
# that grew a new check overnight looks identical to a regression you introduced.
#
# These are manifest-list digests, so they resolve on any architecture.
#
# Bump deliberately, never by drift:
#     make security-bump-scanner-images
#
# That re-resolves each TAG below to its current digest and rewrites this file,
# so the diff shows exactly which scanner moved and when. Review the upstream
# changelog before merging a bump — a new ZAP rule set can change findings on
# unchanged code, which is a real signal but not one you want arriving unnoticed.
#
# Freshness note: pinning the dependency-check *image* does not pin its CVE data.
# That lives in the NVD database and is governed separately by
# DEPENDENCY_CHECK_MAX_DB_AGE_DAYS — see docs/dependencies.md. The two mechanisms
# are deliberately independent: the tool should be reproducible, the data should
# be current, and conflating them gets one of the two wrong.

# Resolved 2026-09-09
ZAP_IMAGE_TAG="ghcr.io/zaproxy/zaproxy:stable"
ZAP_IMAGE_DIGEST="sha256:781a2bdaea47324e7bab583e2263f21d257b0aee61ed51521a5be45f5f5081ef"

DEPENDENCY_CHECK_IMAGE_TAG="docker.io/owasp/dependency-check:latest"
DEPENDENCY_CHECK_IMAGE_DIGEST="sha256:0e8f69ad5b00d62000937f4e57189812343f6903d9c83ee712012d51542a020a"
